Example 1: Sort Integers in Ascending Order
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> list = Arrays.asList(5, 2, 8, 1, 9);

        list.sort((a, b) -> a - b);

        System.out.println(list);
    }
}

Output:

[1, 2, 5, 8, 9]
Example 2: Sort Integers in Descending Order
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> list = Arrays.asList(5, 2, 8, 1, 9);

        list.sort((a, b) -> b - a);

        System.out.println(list);
    }
}

Output:

[9, 8, 5, 2, 1]
Example 3: Sort Strings by Length
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> words = Arrays.asList("Java", "C", "Python", "Go");

        words.sort((a, b) -> a.length() - b.length());

        System.out.println(words);
    }
}

Output:

[C, Go, Java, Python]
Example 4: Sort Objects
import java.util.*;

class Student {
    String name;
    int age;

    Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Student> students = new ArrayList<>();

        students.add(new Student("John", 22));
        students.add(new Student("Alice", 20));
        students.add(new Student("Bob", 21));

        students.sort((s1, s2) -> s1.age - s2.age);

        for (Student s : students) {
            System.out.println(s.name + " " + s.age);
        }
    }
}

Output:

Alice 20
Bob 21
John 22
Using Comparator.comparing()

Sort by name:

students.sort(Comparator.comparing(s -> s.name));

Sort by age:

students.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(s -> s.age));

Descending order:

students.sort(Comparator.comparingInt((Student s) -> s.age).reversed());
Multiple-Level Sorting

Sort by age, then by name:

students.sort(
    Comparator.comparingInt((Student s) -> s.age)
              .thenComparing(s -> s.name)
);
Comparator vs Comparable
Feature	Comparator	Comparable
Package	java.util	java.lang
Method	compare(T o1, T o2)	compareTo(T o)
Sorting	External/custom	Natural/default
Multiple sorting orders	✅ Yes	❌ No
Best Practice

When comparing numeric values, avoid subtraction (a - b) because it can overflow for large integers. Prefer:

list.sort(Integer::compare);

or

students.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(s -> s.age));

These are safer and more readable.

Time Complexity

Sorting with a comparator uses Java's sorting algorithm:

Time Complexity: O(n log n)
Space Complexity: Typically O(n) for object sorting (TimSort).
